<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_doi"><span class="ltx_note_name">DOI: </span><a href="https://doi.org/XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX">XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX</a></span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_ccs"><span class="ltx_note_name">CCS: </span>Computing methodologies Cooperation and coordination</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Guochen Yan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Peking University</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Luyuan Xie
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Peking University</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qingni Shen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Peking University</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuejian Fang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Peking University</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhonghai Wu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Peking University</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">© none</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The current paradigm of training large language models (LLMs) on publicly available Web data is becoming unsustainable, with high-quality data sources in specialized domains nearing exhaustion. Federated Learning (FL) emerges as a practical solution for the next generation of AI on a decentralized Web, enabling privacy-preserving collaborative fine-tuning by leveraging private data distributed across a global client base. While Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) is the standard for efficient fine-tuning, its application in federated settings presents a critical challenge: communication overhead remains a significant bottleneck across the Web’s heterogeneous network conditions. The structural redundancy within LoRA parameters not only incurs a heavy communication burden but also introduces conflicts when aggregating client updates. To address this, we propose FedSRD, a Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose framework designed for communication-efficient FL. We first introduce an importance-aware sparsification method that preserves the structural integrity of LoRA updates to reduce the uploaded parameter count. The server then reconstructs and aggregates these updates in a full-rank space to mitigate conflicts. Finally, it decomposes the global update into a sparse low-rank format for broadcast, ensuring a symmetrically efficient cycle. We also propose an efficient variant, FedSRD-e, to reduce computational overhead. Experimental results on 10 benchmarks demonstrate that our framework significantly reduces communication costs by up to 90% while even improving model performance on heterogeneous client data.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Federated Learning, Large Language Models, Communication, LoRA, Fine-tuning
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs), pre-trained on vast web-scale corpora, have demonstrated remarkable generalist capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. However, realizing their full potential for specialized downstream applications requires fine-tuning on diverse, high-quality, domain-specific data. Such data is often proprietary and distributed across numerous private sources, resulting in fragmented "data silos." As centralizing this sensitive data is frequently impractical due to privacy regulations and cost constraints, Federated Learning (FL) offers a compelling solution, enabling multiple clients to collaboratively fine-tune a global shared LLM without direct data exchange. This paradigm is central to building the next generation of intelligent, decentralized Web applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While FL provides a privacy-preserving solution, fine-tuning billion-parameter LLMs across distributed clients remains computationally and communicatively prohibitive for individual participants. To overcome this, Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods, particularly Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, have become the standard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, LoRA significantly reduces the training burden by freezing the pre-trained model weights and optimizing only a small set of trainable, low-rank matrices. In a federated setting, clients train and communicate only these LoRA parameters, achieving performance comparable to full-parameter fine-tuning at a fraction of the resource cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the efficiency of LoRA, the iterative communication of its parameters remains a substantial overhead. This becomes a critical bottleneck when deployed across the highly heterogeneous and unpredictable network conditions characteristic of the global Web and mobile systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dorfman et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mundt, 2004</a>)</cite>. High communication costs can exclude clients with limited bandwidth, such as those in rural areas or on mobile devices, undermining data diversity and posing fairness and inclusivity challenges for the global Web ecosystem. While sparsification is a common strategy for reducing communication, conventional methods are ill-adapted for federated LoRA fine-tuning on non-IID data. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, naïve approaches like magnitude-based pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aji and Heafield, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yadav et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deep et al., 2024</a>)</cite> disrupt the critical structural relationship between the LoRA matrices <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, while random sparsification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> also leads to performance degradation at high sparsity ratios. Crucially, both methods fail to preserve client-specific knowledge and exacerbate conflicts during server-side aggregation under data heterogeneity. This results in an undesirable trade-off: reduced communication at the expense of significant performance loss. This leads us to our central research question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How can we significantly reduce communication costs in federated LLMs fine-tuning without sacrificing model performance under heterogeneous client data?</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04601v1/FL_demo.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/250;" width="476" height="250"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>An illustration of Federated LoRA Fine-tuning. Clients train and communicate only the LoRA matrices. However, this payload can still amount to hundreds of megabytes per round, posing a significant bottleneck.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04601v1/pruning_performance_comparison.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/286;" width="429" height="286"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Performance comparison under different sparsity ratios. Both random and magnitude-based sparsification suffer performance decline at high sparsity ratios, motivating the need for a more advanced, structure-aware strategy.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address this question, we introduce FedSRD, a novel Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose framework for communication-efficient federated LLM fine-tuning. Our framework is designed to maintain high model performance through three sequential operations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">First, on the client side, we develop an importance-aware <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sparsification</span> strategy. Instead of relying on parameter magnitude, we calculate the importance scores that quantify the contribution of each element in the update matrices (<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A</annotation></semantics></math>) to the overall low-rank update (<math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>). This effectively preserves the critical structural interplay between the LoRA matrices. Furthermore, recognizing that different layers in an LLM serve distinct functions, FedSRD adaptively determines the sparsity ratio for each layer based on the kurtosis of its importance score distribution. Aggressive pruning is applied where high importance is concentrated in a few parameters, while conservative pruning is adopted where importance is more diffuse. Second, the server executes the <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reconstruction</span> and <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposition</span>. Upon receiving sparse updates, the server first reconstructs each client’s full-rank weight update (<math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>) before aggregation. This enables aggregation to occur in the full-rank parameter space, a process that is mathematically sound and effectively mitigates the update conflicts arising from non-IID data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Finally, to create a symmetrically efficient communication cycle, the server performs a novel <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposition</span> of the aggregated global update back into a sparse, low-rank format for clients to download. Using a Taylor expansion-based approximation, we employ an alternating scheme that solves for and transmits only one sparse low-rank matrix per round. Furthermore, for scenarios where server-side computation is the bottleneck (e.g., on edge servers), we propose an efficient variant, FedSRD-e, which bypasses the computationally intensive SVD projection during decomposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate the effectiveness of our framework through extensive experiments that simulate practical non-IID scenarios by finetuning an LLM with clients from diverse specialist domains. Our evaluation on 8 in-domain and 2 out-of-domain benchmarks shows that FedSRD reduces both upload and download communication by 90% while achieving even better performance. In summary, our contributions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel, structure-aware sparsification method for federated LoRA fine-tuning based on importance scores. It effectively identifies and preserves the most salient parameters, overcoming the limitations of conventional sparsification methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a two-step server-side process: (1) reconstructing and aggregating client updates in a full-rank space to ensure mathematically sound aggregation and conflict mitigation, and (2) an alternating decomposition method to create sparse global updates for efficient download. We also introduce an efficient variant of this process to reduce server computation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments demonstrating that our methods can save over 90% of communication costs while achieving superior model performance, validating FedSRD as a practical and high-performing solution for federated LLMs fine-tuning on the Web.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The large scale of modern LLMs makes the full-parameter fine-tuning computationally prohibitive in many resource-constrained environments, such as on mobile or edge devices. To address this, Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>)</cite> have been developed, which freeze most of a pre-trained model’s parameters and only update a small part. Current approaches include adapter-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edalati et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Houlsby et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> that insert small, trainable modules between model layers, and prompting methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petrov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lester et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, such as prefix-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Liang, 2021</a>)</cite>, that add learnable tokens to the input sequence. Among these, Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> has emerged as a dominant and effective approach. The key insight of LoRA is that weight updates during fine-tuning possess a low intrinsic rank. LoRA represents the update for each target weight matrix as the product of two smaller, trainable low-rank matrices, significantly reducing the number of trainable parameters and associated memory footprint while achieving comparable performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Federated LLMs Fine-tuning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Federated Learning (FL) enables the collaborative fine-tuning of LLMs on decentralized private data, addressing both data scarcity and privacy concerns. A common approach is to combine PEFT methods with standard aggregation algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Recent studies point out that this naive combination introduces aggregation inaccuracy, where separately averaging the decomposed LoRA matrices (<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>) from clients is not mathematically equivalent to averaging the true underlying model updates (<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA</annotation></semantics></math>). Several advanced methods have been proposed to mitigate this issue. FFA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> ensures a mathematically sound aggregation by freezing the LoRA <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices and only aggregating the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. FLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> stacks clients’ LoRA matrices to ensure accurate aggregation. FlexLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>)</cite> proposes to aggregate the true model updates (<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA</annotation></semantics></math>) on the server and then employs Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) to derive new global low-rank matrices for clients. FedICU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., [n. d.]</a>)</cite> disentangles client updates into consensus and divergence components, which are then aggregated separately.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Communication-Efficient Federated LLMs Finetuning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Communication overhead remains a primary bottleneck in FL systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sattler et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, a problem that is exacerbated when fine-tuning LLMs over the public internet. While PEFT methods provide a baseline level of efficiency, further optimizations are crucial. Structured methods impose constraints on the LoRA updates. For instance, FFA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> halves the communication cost by transmitting only the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. Building on this, FedSVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a>)</cite> iteratively re-initializes LoRA <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. Fed-SB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singhal et al., 2025</a>)</cite> freezes the LoRA matrices entirely and instead inserts a small, trainable square matrix for aggregation. While these approaches reduce communication, they often do so at the cost of model performance by constraining the expressive capacity of LoRA. Other lines of work focus on generic compression. Quantization methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aji and Heafield, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Konečnỳ et al., 2016</a>)</cite> represent parameters with fewer bits, but they often fail to achieve high compression ratios and can lead to significant accuracy degradation, particularly in non-IID settings. Sparsification methods, which transmit only a subset of non-zero parameters, can achieve much higher compression ratios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aji and Heafield, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Yet, traditional approaches are not directly applicable to LoRA. Magnitude-based pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aji and Heafield, 2017</a>)</cite> is suboptimal because it ignores the coupled relationship between the two LoRA matrices. Similarly, random sparsification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> fails to preserve critical parameters, resulting in inferior performance at high sparsity ratios.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>LoRA in LLMs Fine-tuning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LoRA has been proven to exhibit better performance than other PEFT methods in the federated learning system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. LoRA constrains the weight update in low-rank space:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}+\Delta W=W_{0}+BA," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}+\Delta W=W_{0}+BA,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the frozen model weights, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> are paired low-rank trainable matrices inserted in the linear weights to approximate the update <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\times d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(d_{out},d_{in})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(d_{out},d_{in})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized to zero and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized with random Gaussian noises <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider an FL system with one server and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> clients. Each client has its local private dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{i}=\{x_{j}^{i},y_{j}^{i}\}_{j=1}^{|\mathcal{D}^{i}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{i}=\{x_{j}^{i},y_{j}^{i}\}_{j=1}^{|\mathcal{D}^{i}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. These datasets may originate from different domains or tasks, creating a heterogeneous (non-IID) scenario. All clients collaboratively fine-tune a shared LLM <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to perform well on their respective data distributions. This can be formulated as the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\mathcal{M}}\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{M})=\sum_{i}^{m}\mathcal{L}_{i}(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{D}^{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\mathcal{M}}\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{M})=\sum_{i}^{m}\mathcal{L}_{i}(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{D}^{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{D}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{D}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the loss of the LLM <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on local dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the recent studies, we adopt LoRA to fine-tune the LLM <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in local fine-tuning on each client. The local optimization of client <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> could be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\mathcal{L}_{i}=\min_{\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M})}\mathbb{E}_{(x_{j}^{i},y_{j}^{i})\sim\mathcal{D}^{i}}L(x_{j}^{i},y_{j}^{i},\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\mathcal{L}_{i}=\min_{\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M})}\mathbb{E}_{(x_{j}^{i},y_{j}^{i})\sim\mathcal{D}^{i}}L(x_{j}^{i},y_{j}^{i},\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the trainable parameters set of LoRA. <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the cross-entroy loss on completion <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Proposed Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce FedSRD, a Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose framework for federated LoRA fine-tuning, illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. FedSRD establishes a symmetrically efficient communication cycle by transmitting only sparse updates of the LoRA parameters. At each communication round <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, a client <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> first computes its local LoRA updates <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, then uses our proposed importance-aware strategy to sparsify them for upload (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Importance-aware Sparsification ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). The server then reconstructs each client’s full weight matrix and aggregates them in the full-rank space to mitigate non-IID conflicts (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Reconstruct to Aggregation ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Finally, to ensure an efficient download, the server decomposes the resulting global update into a single sparse low-rank matrix for broadcast using an alternating, Taylor approximation-based method (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Alternative Decomposition ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). The pseudo-code is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Pseudo-Code of FedSRD and FedSRD-e ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04601v1/framework.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/243;" width="476" height="243"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>The FedSRD framework. (1) Client-Side sparsification: Each client computes its LoRA updates <math id="S4.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and uses our importance-aware sparsification to generate sparse updates <math id="S4.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B_{i,s}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B_{i,s}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A_{i,s}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A_{i,s}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for upload. (2) Server-Side reconstruction to aggregation: The server reconstructs each client’s full weight matrix <math id="S4.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and aggregates them in the full-rank space. (3) Server-Side decomposition: The server computes the global update <math id="S4.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then decomposed into a single sparse matrix for efficient download.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Importance-aware Sparsification</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce upload communication costs, sparsification is a natural approach. However, mainstream methods are ill-suited for federated LoRA fine-tuning. Magnitude-based sparsification is ineffective as it prunes each LoRA matrix independently, ignoring the crucial structural interplay between <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. On non-IID data, random-based sparsification fails to preserve performance at high sparsity ratios because it cannot differentiate between critical and unimportant parameters. This motivates a sparsification method that can (1) account for the structural relationship between <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and (2) robustly preserve the most salient parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose importance-aware sparsification. Instead of transmitting the full LoRA matrices, we transmit only their updates at each round. Specifically, on client <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at round <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we first conduct LoRA fine-tuning on its local dataset to obtain the new LoRA matrices <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{t}=B_{i}^{t-1}+\Delta B_{i}^{t},\quad A_{i}^{t}=A_{i}^{t-1}+\Delta A_{i}^{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{t}=B_{i}^{t-1}+\Delta B_{i}^{t},\quad A_{i}^{t}=A_{i}^{t-1}+\Delta A_{i}^{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are the matrices from the previous round <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              Since our operations are layer independent, we omit layer-specific notations for clarity.</span></span></span>. The full-rank weight update <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta W_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta W_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=B_{i}^{t}A_{i}^{t}-B_{i}^{t-1}A_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=B_{i}^{t}A_{i}^{t}-B_{i}^{t-1}A_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=B_{i}^{t}(A_{i}^{t-1}+\Delta A_{i}^{t})-B_{i}^{t-1}A_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=B_{i}^{t}(A_{i}^{t-1}+\Delta A_{i}^{t})-B_{i}^{t-1}A_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(B_{i}^{t}-B_{i}^{t-1})A_{i}^{t-1}+B_{i}^{t}\Delta A_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(B_{i}^{t}-B_{i}^{t-1})A_{i}^{t-1}+B_{i}^{t}\Delta A_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Delta B_{i}^{t}A_{i}^{t-1}+B_{i}^{t}\Delta A_{i}^{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Delta B_{i}^{t}A_{i}^{t-1}+B_{i}^{t}\Delta A_{i}^{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">This formulation reveals that the true contribution of an element in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the corresponding row in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and likewise for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. To formalize this, we therefore define importance score <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to measure an element’s contribution to the final weight update by scaling its magnitude with the norm of the corresponding vector it interacts with in the other matrix:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\Delta B_{i}^{t}[u,v])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\Delta B_{i}^{t}[u,v])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|\Delta B_{i}^{t}[u,v]|\cdot\|A_{i}^{t-1}[v,:]^{T}\|_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|\Delta B_{i}^{t}[u,v]|\cdot\|A_{i}^{t-1}[v,:]^{T}\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\Delta A_{i}^{t}[u,v])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\Delta A_{i}^{t}[u,v])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|\Delta A_{i}^{t}[u,v]|\cdot\|B_{i}^{t}[:,u]||_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|\Delta A_{i}^{t}[u,v]|\cdot\|B_{i}^{t}[:,u]||_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">A higher importance score indicates a greater contribution to the final weight update. We explain the rationale behind in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Rationale of the Importance Score Calculation ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. We then prune elements with the lowest scores, transmitting only the most important portion of the parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, recognizing that LoRA matrices in different layers play distinct roles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we propose to adaptively assign a sparsity ratio <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to each matrix. The core intuition is to use the kurtosis of the importance score distribution reveals a layer’s functional "prunability". A high kurtosis (a leptokurtic, heavy-tailed distribution) signifies that importance is concentrated in a few parameters, justifying an aggressive sparsity ratio. Conversely, a low kurtosis (a platykurtic, flat distribution) implies that importance is diffused, necessitating a more conservative ratio. The layer-specific sparsity ratio is thus calculated as:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rho_{\Delta B_{i}^{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rho_{\Delta B_{i}^{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha+0.1\cdot\log(\text{kurtosis}(I(\Delta B_{i}^{t})))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>kurtosis</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha+0.1\cdot\log(\text{kurtosis}(I(\Delta B_{i}^{t}))),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rho_{\Delta A_{i}^{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rho_{\Delta A_{i}^{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha+0.1\cdot\log(\text{kurtosis}(I(\Delta A_{i}^{t})))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>kurtosis</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha+0.1\cdot\log(\text{kurtosis}(I(\Delta A_{i}^{t}))),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter. The sparsity ratios are truncated if they exceed a pre-set upper bound to ensure validity. We provide a detailed explanation and justification of this design in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Justification for Equation ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>. We use the adaptive sparsity ratios to prune <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\Delta B_{i,s}^{t})_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\Delta B_{i,s}^{t})_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}(\Delta B_{i}^{t})_{uv}&amp;\text{if }I(\Delta B_{i}^{t})_{uv}&gt;\text{quantile}(I(\Delta B_{i}^{t}),\rho_{\Delta B_{i}^{t}})\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mtext>quantile</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}(\Delta B_{i}^{t})_{uv}&amp;\text{if }I(\Delta B_{i}^{t})_{uv}&gt;\text{quantile}(I(\Delta B_{i}^{t}),\rho_{\Delta B_{i}^{t}})\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\Delta A_{i,s}^{t})_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\Delta A_{i,s}^{t})_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}(\Delta A_{i}^{t})_{uv}&amp;\text{if }I(\Delta A_{i}^{t})_{uv}&gt;\text{quantile}(I(\Delta A_{i}^{t}),\rho_{\Delta A_{i}^{t}})\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mtext>quantile</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}(\Delta A_{i}^{t})_{uv}&amp;\text{if }I(\Delta A_{i}^{t})_{uv}&gt;\text{quantile}(I(\Delta A_{i}^{t}),\rho_{\Delta A_{i}^{t}})\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B_{i,s}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B_{i,s}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A_{i,s}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A_{i,s}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are sparsified matrices for uploading. The client can upload the sparse matrices in a compressed format.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Reconstruct to Aggregation</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most existing federated learning methods like FedAvg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">McMahan et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, when applied to LoRA, average the clients’ LoRA matrices independently. This aggregation is not mathematically sound and introduces unexpected noises because it multiplicative relationship between the matrices and fails to mitigate update conflicts arising from non-IID client data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This motivates us to develop an advanced aggregation method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To enable a mathematically sound aggregation that preserves client knowledge, we propose to first reconstruct each client’s update in the full-rank space. At round <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, upon receiving the sparse updates <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B_{i,s}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B_{i,s}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A_{i,s}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A_{i,s}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> participating clients, the server first computes each client’s new LoRA matrices, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, using Equation <a href="#S4.E4" title="In Motivation ‣ 4.1. Importance-aware Sparsification ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. It then reconstructs each client’s full-rank weight matrix and aggregates them:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i}^{m}B_{i}^{t}A_{i}^{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i}^{m}B_{i}^{t}A_{i}^{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the aggregated LoRA weights in the full-rank parameter space. By performing aggregation in this higher-dimensional space, the client-specific knowledge encoded in <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is properly combined, reducing the likelihood of destructive interference.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Alternative Decomposition</h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After aggregating in the full-rank space, the server must decompose the result back into low-rank matrices for the clients. Previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a>)</cite> often use Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) to construct new dense matrices <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, transmitting these dense matrices would create a severe download bottleneck, negating the benefit of a sparse upload. Naively sparsifying the full state matrices <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> would damage the learned model, and one cannot simply compute an update as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{t}-B^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{t}-B^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> because it does not correctly approximate the global update (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{t}A^{t}-B^{t-1}A^{t-1}\neq(B^{t}-B^{t-1})A^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{t}A^{t}-B^{t-1}A^{t-1}\neq(B^{t}-B^{t-1})A^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>). This motivates a novel decomposition method that can produce a sparse update for download.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to find sparse updates <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that approximate the change from the previous global state <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to the new one <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, we project <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to its <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-rank approximation via SVD:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U,S,V_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U,S,V_{h}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=SVD(W^{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=SVD(W^{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E10Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{t}_{low\_rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{t}_{low\_rank}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=U[:,:r]S[:r,:r]V_{h}[:r,:]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=U[:,:r]S[:r,:r]V_{h}[:r,:],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}_{low\_rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}_{low\_rank}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-rank approximation of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the LoRA rank. This process inherently denoises the aggregated result by discarding less important components and retaining the consensus. Assuming the global states are low-rank, we can state that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists B^{t-1},A^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists B^{t-1},A^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t-1}_{\text{low\_rank}}=B^{t-1}A^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>low_rank</mtext><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t-1}_{\text{low\_rank}}=B^{t-1}A^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we seek <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B^{t},\Delta A^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B^{t},\Delta A^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}_{\text{low\_rank}}=(B^{t-1}+\Delta B^{t})(A^{t-1}+\Delta A^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>low_rank</mtext><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}_{\text{low\_rank}}=(B^{t-1}+\Delta B^{t})(A^{t-1}+\Delta A^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. We can approximate the update using a first-order Taylor expansion:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{}^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{}^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(B^{t-1}+\Delta B^{t})(A^{t-1}+\Delta A^{t})-B^{t-1}A^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(B^{t-1}+\Delta B^{t})(A^{t-1}+\Delta A^{t})-B^{t-1}A^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=B^{t-1}\Delta A^{t}+\Delta B^{t}A^{t-1}+\Delta B^{t}\Delta A^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=B^{t-1}\Delta A^{t}+\Delta B^{t}A^{t-1}+\Delta B^{t}\Delta A^{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx B^{t-1}\Delta A^{t}+\Delta B^{t}A^{t-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx B^{t-1}\Delta A^{t}+\Delta B^{t}A^{t-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where the higher-order term <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B^{t}\Delta A^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B^{t}\Delta A^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is omitted (discussed in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Magnitude of 𝐵^{𝑡-1},𝐴^{𝑡-1} and Δ⁢𝐵^𝑡,Δ⁢𝐴^𝑡 ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>). To solve this underdetermined system, we alternate between setting one of the deltas to zero. In even-numbered rounds, we set <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B^{t}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B^{t}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and solve for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank}=B^{t-1}\Delta A^{t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank}=B^{t-1}\Delta A^{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta A^{t}=(B^{t-1})^{\dagger}(W^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta A^{t}=(B^{t-1})^{\dagger}(W^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B^{t-1})^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B^{t-1})^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse. In odd-numbered rounds, we set <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{t}=\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{t}=\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and solve for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E13X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank}=\Delta B^{t}A^{t-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank}=\Delta B^{t}A^{t-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E13Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta B^{t}=(W^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank})(A^{t-1})^{\dagger}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta B^{t}=(W^{t}_{low\_rank}-W^{t-1}_{low\_rank})(A^{t-1})^{\dagger},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">The resulting single update matrix (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) can be simply sparsified using random pruning and rescaled <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to produce a sparse update (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta A^{t}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A^{t}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta B_{s}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta B_{s}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) for download. Clients receive this single sparse matrix and update their local model accordingly, completing the communication-efficient cycle.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">FedSRD-e: a Computationally Efficient Variant</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For scenarios where server-side computation is the primary bottleneck, such as in edge devices, we propose a computationally efficient variant. This variant, FedSRD-e, bypasses the SVD projection and instead directly approximates the full-rank global update, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{t}=W^{t}-W^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{t}=W^{t}-W^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This matrix <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> replaces <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{t}_{\text{low\_rank}}-W^{t-1}_{\text{low\_rank}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>low_rank</mtext><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mtext>low_rank</mtext><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{t}_{\text{low\_rank}}-W^{t-1}_{\text{low\_rank}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the left-hand side of Equation <a href="#S4.E11" title="In Motivation ‣ 4.3. Alternative Decomposition ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. The subsequent steps of solving for a single update matrix remain the same. This variant significantly reduces computation by avoiding the costly SVD operation. It only consumes slightly more time than the simple FedAvg method, serving as a powerful, high-speed alternative.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Remark</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that both the reconstruction and decomposition operations are layer-independent, meaning they can be performed layer by layer. Thus, the additional memory resource demand on the server is negligible.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Details of the local datasets used by each client. All datasets are from the training split.</figcaption>
