<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We simulate an FL system with 4 clients from distinct specialist domains: Code, Medical, Finance, and Math. We use CodeAlpaca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chaudhary, 2023</a>)</cite>, the training set of MedQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, Finance Alpaca<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              https://huggingface.co/datasets/gbharti/finance-alpaca</span></span></span>, and a combination of the training sets from GMS8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite> as the local datasets for these clients, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our framework FedSRD and FedSRD-e with 6 state-of-the-art federated learning methods: (1) FedAvg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">McMahan et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, (2) FedProx <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, (3) FFA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which freezes LoRA <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrcies to ensure efficient communication and effective aggregation, (4) FlexLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which aggregates in full-rank space and uses SVD for download, (5) FedICU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., [n. d.]</a>)</cite>, which selectively updates on global model and perform consensus-divergence splitting in the server-side aggregatio, (6) Fed-DARE, which applies random sparsification with rescaling (DARE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) to LoRA updates for upload. A detailed description of baselines is in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Description of Baselines ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3. </span>Benchmarks</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate baselines and our methods on 10 benchmarks, including 8 in-domain benchmarks from Code, Medical, Finance, and Math domains, and 2 out-of-domain benchmarks to evaluate the knowledge retention of other domains during fine-tuning. We provide details of these benchmarks in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Remark ‣ 4.3. Alternative Decomposition ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.4. </span>Implementation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we adopt Llama3.2-3B <span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.2-3B</span></span></span> and Qwen2-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite> as base models for federated fine-tuning over 50 rounds. We apply LoRA to all linear layers with rank <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=64</annotation></semantics></math> for Llama3.2-3B and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=32</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen2-7B. The learning rate is tuned from 1e-5 to 2e-4. For Fed-DARE, the sparsity ratio is 0.9 as suggested <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. For our method, we tune <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in Equation <a href="#S4.E7" title="In Motivation ‣ 4.1. Importance-aware Sparsification ‣ 4. Proposed Method ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> within [0.85, 0.9] and set the download sparsity ratio to 0.8. For other hyperparameters, we follow the suggested values from the original papers. We report average performance on in-domain and out-of-domain benchmarks, as well as the per-round communication cost per client. We conduct training on NVIDIA A800 GPUs and inference on NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPUs with bfloat16 precision.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Comparison of model performance and per-round communication cost using Llama3.2-3B as the base model. OOD represents Out-of-Domain. The best and second-best results are highlighted in bold and underline, respectively.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:63.8pt;vertical-align:-30.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-190.7pt,35.3pt) scale(0.47494781122226,0.47494781122226) ;">
<table id="S5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" rowspan="3"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" rowspan="3"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comm.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(MB)</span></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="8">In-Domain</td>
