<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Experimental Results</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1. </span>Performance Comparison</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main results, reported in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1.4. Implementation ‣ 5.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (Llama3.2-3B) and Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.1.4. Implementation ‣ 5.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (Qwen2-7B), comprehensively demonstrate the effectiveness and efficiency of our FedSRD framework. We analyze performance from three key perspectives.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">In-Domain Performance.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across the experiments, our proposed methods, FedSRD and FedSRD-e, consistently achieve state-of-the-art performance on in-domain benchmarks. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1.4. Implementation ‣ 5.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with the Llama3.2-3B model, FedSRD and FedSRD-e achieve the highest average in-domain performance score, outperforming all baseline methods. This trend is further confirmed with the larger Qwen2-7B model in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.1.4. Implementation ‣ 5.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, where FedSRD and FedSRD-e again lead with top performance, respectively. While some baselines may achieve peak performance on specific benchmarks, they often falter in other domains. In contrast, our framework exhibits robust and consistently high performance across all four specialized domains, underscoring its superior capability in aggregating diverse knowledge from specialist clients to build powerful global LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Communication Efficiency.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A standout advantage of our framework is its remarkable communication efficiency. FedSRD and FedSRD-e reduce the per-round communication overhead to just 74 MB for Llama3.2-3B and 61 MB for Qwen2-7B. This represents a reduction of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> 90% compared to standard methods like FedAvg (742 MB) and advanced methods like FlexLoRA (742 MB). This reduction is achieved by our proposed <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">importance-aware sparsification</span> for uploads and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">alternative decomposition</span> for downloads (see Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Communication Cost Analysis ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for a detailed analysis). Crucially, this significant gain in efficiency does not come at the cost of performance. On the contrary, FedSRD and FedSRD-e achieve higher performance than baselines, directly answering our central research question. This is visualized in Figures <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1.4. Implementation ‣ 5.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1.4. Implementation ‣ 5.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, where our methods are shown to be Pareto optimal: achieving the best performance with the least communication.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Out-of-Domain Performance.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our methods also demonstrate strong performance on out-of-domain (OOD) benchmarks, indicating effective mitigation of catastrophic forgetting. For Llama3.2-3B, FedSRD and FedSRD-e achieve the leading OOD performance. For Qwen2-7B, they also maintain competitive performance. Although FFA-LoRA also exhibits strong OOD retention, we attribute this to its mechanism of freezing the LoRA <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. which inherently constrains the capacity and hinders new knowledge acquisition in favor of preserving prior information. In contrast, our methods provide a more balanced and consistently high level of performance across both in-domain and out-of-domain evaluations. Further, we posit that the SVD projection in FedSRD, by seeking a low-rank consensus for in-domain tasks, may inadvertently filter out some general knowledge from the base model, which could account for the superior OOD performance of FedSRD-e. In summary, our framework not only excels at integrating specialized knowledge but also effectively preserves the general capabilities of the base model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2. </span>Abation Study</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate each component of our FedSRD framework, we conducted an ablation study on Llama3.2-3B, with results in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.2.2. Abation Study ‣ 5.2. Experimental Results ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Starting with the full model, we sequentially removed or replaced key components. Disabling the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">adaptive sparsity ratio</span> (using a fixed ratio instead) causes a slight performance drop, affirming the benefit of dynamic adjustment. Replacing our <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">importance-aware sparsification</span> with a random baseline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> leads to a significant degradation, highlighting that our decomposition method effectively preserves aggregated knowledge while reducing download communication costs. Further, ablating our <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">alternative decomposition</span> (reverting to dense SVD download) also harms performance, confirming its effectiveness in preserving knowledge while reducing download costs. Finally, removing the reconstruction step (reverting to naive LoRA matrix averaging) results in severe performance loss, proving that full-rank aggregation is crucial for mitigating client heterogeneity. These results confirm that each component plays an indispensable and synergistic role in achieving the dual objectives of high model performance and extreme communication efficiency. Additionally, we show in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Importance-aware Sparsification Suits Well for FedAvg ‣ FedSRD: Sparsify-Reconstruct-Decompose for Communication-Efficient Federated Large Language Models Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> that our importance-aware sparsification also works as a more effective plugin to enhance conventional methods like FedAvg.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
